    "question": {
      ":question/id": "62cdd465-33fb-5502-96e7-4dd38ffccd62",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\sqrt{{y}^{′}+x}=3{y}^{4}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
