      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **II.** ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
